  "explanation": [
    "With $u = 2x$: $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\sin(2x) \\, dx = \\frac{1}{2}\\int_0^{\\pi} \\sin u \\, du$.",
    "$= \\frac{1}{2}[-\\cos u]_0^{\\pi} = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(-(-1) - (-1)\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}(2)$.",
    "$= 1$. One full arch of $\\sin(2x)$ has area $1$ — half the area $2$ of an arch of $\\sin x$, because it is squeezed to half the width."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Guess and adjust",
      "steps": [
        "A first guess for the antiderivative is $-\\cos(2x)$. Differentiating gives $2\\sin(2x)$ — twice too big.",
        "So the antiderivative is $-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos(2x)$.",
        "Evaluate: $-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos \\pi + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 0 = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = 1$."
      ]
    }
